#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7b08d2-6aa3-571d-bce0-f9f5b6834e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361657} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e22241-9ebb-5abe-926d-904c3d94a437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I-\\frac{1}{6}A+\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361658} {:answer/id #uuid "16023665-e33b-5d9a-afa5-f419d0d7b5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{6}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361659} {:answer/id #uuid "8b85c38f-37a1-53a0-b59f-11216cdc771e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{12}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361660} {:answer/id #uuid "64c0d39d-9c35-5ced-96c0-3594d2ccc28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}I+\\frac{1}{3}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c7b08d2-6aa3-571d-bce0-f9f5b6834e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361657} :answer-field/id #uuid "846946f0-267b-5b49-9625-b464e537521c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7a4964c-4a39-54df-8b48-fb778fd1712f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140531" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-2A-11I+24{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 24{A}^{-1} & =11I+2A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f4e5ff7-e5cc-533c-969e-4ccd1b2237a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12A+16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366588} {:answer/id #uuid "8204610c-8297-5f5e-a28f-e060f549400c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12A-16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366589} {:answer/id #uuid "d20e6c30-429b-57a9-a623-22ef4976599c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2A+6I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366590} {:answer/id #uuid "49cc11cf-2d4f-5671-a802-b16f2aef160b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16A-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366591} {:answer/id #uuid "381b3feb-4fe1-52f1-9017-34bc0b08d4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2A+I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4e5ff7-e5cc-533c-969e-4ccd1b2237a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12A+16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366588} :answer-field/id #uuid "800a0099-1ff2-5ceb-afe7-173cdf1dc52f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8267e9e-8ead-5bd0-9650-b3898b28bfc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38705" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-2x-8,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-2x-8,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $+$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-2x-8\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $2{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $8x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | $2{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $8x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | $2{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $4x$ | $-$ | $16$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $12x$ | $+$ | $16$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(12x+16),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x+2.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(12A+16I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(12A+16I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(12A+16I) \\\\  & =12A+16I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a173e5ae-9eb8-5760-b521-013a149f20bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2A^{2} - A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123359} {:answer/id #uuid "bf49fa25-1ee9-52d5-bd11-6ce0f81d247c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2A^{2} - I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123360} {:answer/id #uuid "c99d75d8-f575-5589-9103-a61e4585d8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2A - I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123361} {:answer/id #uuid "db25d64d-a845-56f6-86ae-6bf7780e22e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2A - I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123362} {:answer/id #uuid "df6a7377-9668-54d3-a708-c897eeecfbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2A + A^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db25d64d-a845-56f6-86ae-6bf7780e22e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2A - I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123362} :answer-field/id #uuid "57acdcc3-f2aa-5d70-8a56-3f5947d2049d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "241347e1-d70b-502b-97bb-5ffa2634f0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38723" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=-{\\lambda }^{3}-2\\lambda -1.$ Then, ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{3}-2A-I & =O \\\\ {A}^{3} & =-2A-I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f3c81fc-033e-51ba-b526-ae616d892ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & -22 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366595} {:answer/id #uuid "fc299d90-000e-50ee-bbd1-d1585983c19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366596} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b3a671-a720-5cf1-9028-05d42dcc7a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366597} {:answer/id #uuid "57851f5c-54c8-56d8-acd2-67503e02eeb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -22 \\\\ 18 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366598} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd3035-168f-5d50-93ab-043dc6373700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b3a671-a720-5cf1-9028-05d42dcc7a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366597} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63e164a-d595-58e8-92fb-306fad4dbacc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c6e1cb3-d3e5-5ac7-9b61-9f6c0c45ae57" :question/math-academy-id "q-38728" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(6-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )+33 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{13}$ | $+$ | $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-3\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{15}$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{13}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,5x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$ |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,3x$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,2x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(-2x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{13}+x.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =-2A+6I \\\\  & =-2[\\begin{aligned}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]+6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db10fe87-4080-51de-81c0-15a642191abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{3}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361678} {:answer/id #uuid "222aa5b5-eb2f-5362-bffe-6ddf95ce05f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361679} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd1e470-e2e8-51f8-bac3-5bae9ed53e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2I-A-2{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361680} {:answer/id #uuid "27704e3e-cb96-5838-9059-1fd29d8363bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{7}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361681} {:answer/id #uuid "241ae2fa-cdf3-5aad-81c7-7048c5a0658a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I-A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222aa5b5-eb2f-5362-bffe-6ddf95ce05f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361679} :answer-field/id #uuid "09ff4040-4ecd-5896-9de2-3cf9ce932e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ef480b2-61d6-5846-831c-c9cbf5e226fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40168" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+A-7I+2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =7I-A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
